sellingPoints:[{title:"Opening pitch",text:"A classic cruiser with modern tech underneath. Twin exhausts, USD forks, belt drive - under $11k."},{title:"Vs competitors",text:"It competes with the Honda Rebel 500 and Kawasaki Vulcan S, both popular choices. The Chinchilla stands out with USD forks, which is upside-down sportsbike suspension on a cruiser at this price. Add twin exhausts for the sound and the look, and it feels more premium than either rival. At $9,990 it's also priced to win."},{title:"Ideal customer",text:"Rider who loves traditional cruiser aesthetics. Wants to look and feel like a cruiser rider on weekends."},{title:"What's different from the NB500?",text:"The engine is the same, identical 475cc V-twin with the same power. The difference is everything else. The Chinchilla is wider, lower, and more relaxed to sit on. It has twin exhausts that sound richer, and USD forks that handle better under braking. Sit them on both, the choice usually becomes obvious in about 30 seconds."}],
anchors:{type:"A relaxed, low-slung style built for comfort. Feet-forward riding position, wide handlebars and a wide, planted stance.",feel:"Low and planted in traffic and happy on a longer run. The upside-down forks add sharper handling than most cruisers deliver.",who:"Anyone who loves the classic cruiser look. Easy enough for new riders, satisfying enough for experienced ones."},
